    <!-- Analysis Results - Go Gildong -->
    <string name="go_gildong_summary">The employment contract has several issues including unspecified probationary period and wages, and no break time mentioned. However, it complies with minimum wage and basic working conditions are specified, requiring attention.</string>
    <string name="go_gildong_highlight_1">Probationary period and wages not specified</string>
    <string name="go_gildong_highlight_2">Break time not specified</string>
    <string name="go_gildong_highlight_3">Foreign worker accommodation type and meal costs not specified</string>
    <string name="go_gildong_legal_issue_1">Obligation to specify probationary period and wages</string>
    <string name="go_gildong_legal_interpretation_1">According to Labor Standards Act Article 17, probationary period and wage composition and calculation method must be clearly specified.</string>
    <string name="go_gildong_legal_issue_2">Break time guarantee</string>
    <string name="go_gildong_legal_interpretation_2">According to Labor Standards Act Article 54, at least 30 minutes of break time must be provided for 4 hours of work.</string>
    <string name="go_gildong_legal_issue_3">Foreign worker support obligations</string>
    <string name="go_gildong_legal_interpretation_3">According to the Act on Foreign Workers Employment, accommodation and meal conditions must be specified.</string>
    <string name="go_gildong_deep_analysis">This contract meets most basic requirements of the Labor Standards Act, but attention is needed due to several important omissions.\n\nThe omission of probationary period is an important matter related to wage deduction. According to Labor Standards Act Article 17, the probationary period must be specified in the employment contract, and omitting this may result in workers working under different conditions than expected.\n\nThe omission of break time directly affects the protection of workers\' rights. Labor Standards Act Article 54 requires guaranteeing at least 30 minutes of break time for 4 or more hours of work, and failure to specify this may result in insufficient rest during actual work.\n\nFor foreign workers, accommodation type and meal cost conditions are important working conditions. According to the Act on Foreign Workers Employment, these conditions must be clearly specified, and omission can cause disputes.\n\nFortunately, minimum wage standards are met, so there are no serious legal violations, but the above omissions need to be addressed to create a more complete contract.</string>
    
    <!-- Analysis Results - Kwak Deoksik -->
    <string name="kwak_deoksik_summary">The employment contract has a basic framework but may have issues with worker rights protection due to unclear probationary period and wage payment conditions.</string>
    <string name="kwak_deoksik_highlight_1">Probationary period not specified</string>
    <string name="kwak_deoksik_highlight_2">Wage payment method unclear</string>
    <string name="kwak_deoksik_highlight_3">Annual paid leave conditions not specified</string>
    <string name="kwak_deoksik_legal_issue_1">Probationary period regulations</string>
    <string name="kwak_deoksik_legal_interpretation_1">According to Labor Standards Act Article 2-2, probationary period cannot exceed 3 months and must be specified in the contract.</string>
    <string name="kwak_deoksik_legal_issue_2">Wage payment method</string>
    <string name="kwak_deoksik_legal_interpretation_2">According to Labor Standards Act Article 43, wages must be paid in full and directly to workers in currency, and payment method must be clearly specified.</string>
    <string name="kwak_deoksik_legal_issue_3">Annual paid leave</string>
    <string name="kwak_deoksik_legal_interpretation_3">According to Labor Standards Act Article 60, 15 days of paid leave must be granted to workers who attended 80% or more for one year.</string>
    <string name="kwak_deoksik_deep_analysis">This contract follows the basic structure of the Labor Standards Act, but attention is needed due to unclear parts in several items important for worker rights protection.\n\nThe non-specification of probationary period is a serious problem. According to Labor Standards Act Article 2-2, probationary period is limited to a maximum of 3 months, and wage reduction is possible during this period, so it must be specified in the contract. If probationary period is not specified, workers may face unexpected wage reduction.\n\nUnclear wage payment method is also problematic. Labor Standards Act Article 43 stipulates paying wages in full and directly to workers in currency, and unclear payment method risks wage arrears or improper deductions.\n\nNon-specification of annual paid leave conditions affects workers\' right to rest. Annual paid leave under Labor Standards Act Article 60 is a basic right of workers, so failure to specify this may prevent proper vacation use in actual work.\n\nOverall, this contract does not seriously violate legal standards, but the above items need to be supplemented for worker protection.</string>
    
    <!-- Analysis Results - Ok Dupal -->
    <string name="ok_dupal_summary">The employment contract has multiple serious legal violations including minimum wage violation, excessive working hours, and unspecified probationary period requiring immediate correction.</string>
    <string name="ok_dupal_highlight_1">Below minimum wage (8,500 won per hour)</string>
    <string name="ok_dupal_highlight_2">Excessive 13-hour daily working hours</string>
    <string name="ok_dupal_highlight_3">Probationary period not specified</string>
    <string name="ok_dupal_legal_issue_1">Minimum Wage Act violation</string>
    <string name="ok_dupal_legal_interpretation_1">According to the Minimum Wage Act, 2024 minimum wage of 9,860 won or more must be paid, but the contract specifies 8,500 won, which is a legal violation.</string>
    <string name="ok_dupal_legal_issue_2">Working hours limit violation</string>
    <string name="ok_dupal_legal_interpretation_2">According to Labor Standards Act Article 50, daily 8 hours and weekly 40 hours cannot be exceeded, but 13-hour work is a serious violation.</string>
    <string name="ok_dupal_legal_issue_3">Probationary period not specified</string>
    <string name="ok_dupal_legal_interpretation_3">According to Labor Standards Act Article 2-2, probationary period must be specified and cannot exceed 3 months maximum.</string>
    <string name="ok_dupal_deep_analysis">This contract seriously violates the Labor Standards Act and is at a dangerous level requiring immediate correction.\n\nThe most serious problem is minimum wage violation. Specifying 8,500 won compared to the 2024 minimum wage of 9,860 won is a clear violation of the Minimum Wage Act, and employers can face up to 3 years imprisonment or fines up to 20 million won. Workers can claim damages for unpaid wages.\n\nExcessive 13-hour daily working hours is also a very serious problem. Labor Standards Act Article 50 sets 8 hours daily and 40 hours weekly as basic working hours, and cannot exceed 52 hours weekly including extended work. 13-hour work greatly violates this and can pose serious risks to worker health and safety.\n\nNon-specification of probationary period is an important matter related to wage reduction. According to Labor Standards Act Article 2-2, probationary period must be specified and cannot exceed 3 months.\n\nThese violations can cause particularly serious harm to foreign workers and require labor office reporting or legal action. Contract conditions must be immediately corrected to meet legal standards.</string>

